//
// SEP SRAM preload: a minimal warm-reset handler for sep_scratch_7_test.
//
// The Boot ROM's warm-reset dispatch (bootrom/prod/src/vector.S) jumps to the
// address in warm_scratch[0] if it lies inside SEP SRAM
// [SEP_SRAM_BASE, SEP_BOOT_ROM_BASE) = [0x10000000, 0x10040000). In production
// that target is BL1, already resident in SRAM. Under test there is no BL1, so
// this file puts one valid instruction at the jump target instead.
//
// WHY A REAL INSTRUCTION AND NOT ZEROS. The check that proves the ROM jumped to
// the *scratch value* (rather than to a hardcoded base, or not at all) is the
// retired-PC trace. SRAM default-fills to zero, and 0x00000000 is an illegal
// instruction: it traps instead of retiring, so it would produce no PC evidence.
// Worse, the ROM's trap_vector needs a valid `sp`, which the warm path never sets
// up (vector.S sets sp in cold_boot, after the warm branch) -- so a trap here
// would corrupt the observation instead of yielding it.
//
// THE INSTRUCTION is `j .` (jump-to-self) = 0x0000006f, verified with
// riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc -march=rv32im_zicsr -mabi=ilp32. It retires forever at
// the entry address, so the trace shows exactly the seeded PC and nothing after.
//
// LAYOUT. u_sep_sram is 32768 x 64-bit, byte-addressed from 0x10000000, and
// $readmemh fills from index 0. The handler entry is 0x10000100 -- deliberately
// NOT offset 0, so "PC == seeded address" cannot be satisfied by a ROM that
// jumped to the range base by mistake. Byte offset 0x100 is 64-bit word 32, so
// words 0..31 are zero filler and word 32 carries the instruction.
//
// Both halves of word 32 hold the instruction. Only the low half (0x10000100) is
// the entry, but filling both makes the preload immune to a half-mapping
// surprise without weakening the PC check -- the ROM still has to jump to
// 0x10000100 for that PC to appear in the trace.
//
// Keep this in sync with _WARM_HANDLER_ADDR in
// cocotb/tests/rom_fw/sep_scratch_7_test.py.
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000000000000000
0000006f0000006f
